        "thinking": "I'm picturing a display stand shaped like a eucalyptus trunk with a base, where the koala sits gripping the fork of the branch with all four limbs.\n\nScaling it realistically, the koala's body is about 0.75m long with a sitting height of 0.6-0.65m. For the stand, I'm sketching a round plinth base (radius 0.45, height 0.08) with a vertical trunk (radius 0.08) rising to about 1.35m, forking at 0.55m where the koala sits with its hindquarters on the lower branch and forelimbs hugging the upper trunk.\n\nTrying another arrangement: trunk rising from the base with the koala leaning its back against it, bum resting on a forked side-branch jutting forward where its legs can straddle and feet grip. But if its back is against the trunk, the arms wrapping around it no longer makes sense, so I need to rethink the pose.\n\nActually a symmetric V-shaped fork works better: two branches rising outward from a fork point, with the koala sitting in the crotch facing forward, arms spread to grip each branch, and legs/feet gripping lower down each side—symmetric and clearly readable as a rest pose.\n\nWorking out the proportions: pelvis around y≈0.6, torso to y≈1.0, head near y≈1.15-1.3, giving a sitting koala height of roughly 0.65m and a total stand height near 1.3-1.35m. I'm checking the arm reach from shoulder position out to where each branch sits at that height, confirming the geometry lines up so the arms can plausibly grip the branches without stretching unnaturally.\n\nFor the paws, I'm planning to wrap digits (two opposable plus three) around the branch circumference so the grip looks believable, and now I'm sketching the base: a pixel-art wood plinth with a darker box body, lighter top slab, moss patches, small eucalyptus leaf cards, and pebbles, considering whether to fake a round log-slice look using stacked octagonal box segments to stay true to the boxy pixel-art style.\n\nI'm also designing a small nameplate on the base front, possibly spelling \"K