1,0.68) and knees further out.\n\nNow I'm working out the leg IK: knee position forward of the branch, shin angling back down to the foot gripping the branch, with thigh and shin segments around 0.2 each easily reaching the hip-to-ankle distance of about 0.14, so the knee bends forward naturally.\n\nI'm also mapping out the branch geometry—the fork base sits below the trunk, with the V-shaped branches running roughly along a shallow plane so the koala's bum nestles right into the fork while its back clears the branch. I'm plotting out a sequence of points along the left branch path curving upward and outward from the fork base.\n\nContinuing to work through tapering branch radii and the slope of the limb near the grip points—figuring out where the hind foot and forepaw actually clamp onto the branch using parametric points along the curve. Now I'm sketching the torso proportions: pelvis around the mid-height, shoulders above that, head near the top, aiming for a realistic sitting koala height of roughly 0.6 meters total.\n\nI'm settling on final body measurements now—pelvis at one height, shoulders a bit higher, head centered slightly forward and tilted, with a deliberately oversized head and ears for a stylized look, giving a total bum-to-head-top span around 0.62. Now I'm working out the forelimb geometry, placing the shoulder joint and sizing the upper arm and forearm segments so the paw can reach the branch's grip point at the right height and offset.\n\nI'm figuring out how the paw grips the branch, wrapping digits around its circumference, and planning a budget of five digits per hand (two thumbs plus three fingers) with claws, while tallying up the overall joint count across spine, head, ears, and arm segments to stay within the 160-joint limit.\n\nWorking out the wrap direction for the grip: palms rest on the front of the branch while fingers curl around to the back side, with thumbs wrapping oppositely since they're opposable.\n\nTo resolve the sign ambigui