e fur tufts.\n\nArms taper with a sweep radius and small paw boxes with digit shapes, fur colored grey outside and cream inside. Legs follow the same tapering pattern with hind feet getting fused digit clusters, hips anchoring at the body root and arms parenting to the chest joint of the spine chain. I'm adding dark claw cones at digit tips, hiding shoulder seams with spheres, and shaping a rounded rump bump at the back of the body.\n\nI'm skipping a tail since koalas just have a stub, and adding pixel-art fur tufts as small scattered cards on the chest and cheeks for texture. For the eucalyptus branch, I'm scattering leaf and gumnut cards along twigs with a cellPaint bark texture in grey-green-white tones.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the stand design, considering a round cut-log slice base using a lathe or tiled pixel boxes, with peeling bark ribbons and dark knot details painted in.\n\nFor the plinth, I'm planning a dark walnut base with a lighter oak top layer, sizing the footprint around 0.9x0.62 to properly support the trunk and leaning branches above, plus adding a small brass plaque reading \"KOALA\" on the front.\n\nI'm also detailing the trunk's connection to the base with a bolted iron collar and hex bolts, scattering mossy tufts, fallen leaves, and gumnuts around the plinth, and shaping the trunk itself as a slightly bent, tapered cylinder rising from the base to the branch fork.\n\nI'm also considering adding a short rear branchlet with leaves behind the koala's head to frame the figure and give the pose a sense of support, though I'm weighing whether it makes the composition too busy.\n\nFor the leaves, I'm thinking about sprouting thin twig sweeps off each branch with leaf cards at the ends, and I need to figure out which joint the stand pieces should bind to since the rig requires everything to trace back to one root. The branch geometry would naturally rig to whatever joint is nearest, likely the hips, but since the stand shouldn't move with the koala's body, I may need a separate static root joint instead.\n\nMy plan is a root joint at the floor as the stand's base, with hips as its child and the koala's chain parented from there, so the stand bone stays fixed while the body chain moves independently. I also need to double check the part budget since each leaf card and texture counts against the 1000-part limit, and I should look at the builder's loft and joint/chain signatures to confirm naming conventions before continuing.\n\n",
